        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61889,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients are typically perimenopausal or postmenopausal and typically present with abdominal swelling or pain.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61890,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are uncommon {24121169; 24525500}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61891,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61892,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These neoplasms arise from endometriosis {6198065; 24121169; 24525500; 28968994} and have genetic alterations similar to those seen in their uterine counterparts, including <em>JAZF1</em>-<em>SUZ12</em> (<em>JJAZ1</em>), <em>EPC1</em>-<em>PHF1</em>, and <em>PHF1</em> rearrangements {21836477; 21316079; 17235569}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61893,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are typically unilateral (with a mean size of 10&nbsp;cm), and most have a solid (sometimes multinodular) or solid and cystic cut surface. The solid areas are soft and uniformly tan to yellow. Rarely, tumours are predominantly cystic, with wall thickening. Haemorrhage and necrosis may be seen {28968994; 24121169; 24525500}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61894,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>A diffuse growth of uniform small cells (sometimes with nodular accentuation) is most commonly seen, sometimes whorling around arteriole-like vessels; however, a fibromatous or oedematous background (mimicking a fibroma) can be found, imparting an alternating cellularity. Smooth muscle, sex cord&ndash;like&nbsp;(mimicking a granulosa cell tumour), and glandular differentiation can occur. Cells are typically small, with scant cytoplasm and bland oval nuclei with inconspicuous nucleoli; mitotic activity is typically low but can be brisk. In areas of smooth muscle differentiation, cells have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm with round to spindled nuclei; areas of sex cord differentiation show cells with abundant vacuolated to eosinophilic cytoplasm and round nuclei. Arteriole-like vessels are common and sometimes hyalinized, but vessels can be curvilinear. Foamy cells and collagen plaques are frequent {24525500; 24121169; 28968994; 6198065; 11603226}. Rare transition to high-grade areas has been reported {24121169}. Tumours often merge with areas of endometriosis {24525500; 24121169; 28968994; 6198065; 11603226}. Unlike in uterine tumours, invasion is only seen in the hilus. These tumours typically express CD10, ER, and PR, with variable expression of keratin, nuclear WT1, and actins. Desmin is positive mostly in areas of smooth muscle differentiation, while melan-A, calretinin, and inhibin are positive in areas of sex cord&ndash;like differentiation {24525500}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:42:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61895,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61896,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61897,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> diffuse growth of uniform small cells, sometimes with whorling around arteriole-like vessels, resembling proliferative-type endometrial stroma; exclusion of a uterine tumour.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> presence of endometriosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61898,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>This entity is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:11:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61899,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 57,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although patients often present with extraovarian disease, these tumours typically have an indolent course and are associated with late recurrences. Surgery is the mainstay of treatment, but radiation and hormonal treatment have also been used {24121169; 24525500}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:00:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]